WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
VOICE OVER: Fossils have driven our&nbsp;
understanding of ancient species for centuries

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.603
but DNA is changing the game.

00:00:07.603 --> 00:00:12.600
ESKE WILLERSLEV: We are walking&nbsp;
around on DNA from the present and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.628
if we dig down, DNA from the past.

00:00:14.628 --> 00:00:19.080
VOICE OVER: Now a team of scientists have&nbsp;
sequenced the oldest DNA ever recovered.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.628
ESKE: Twice the age of what we&nbsp;
previously thought was possible.

00:00:23.628 --> 00:00:28.620
VOICE OVER: The DNA is 2-million-years-
old and from it they've reconstructed

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.980
a detailed and surprising&nbsp;
picture of an ancient world.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.000
ESKE: We could basically see&nbsp;
this whole ecosystem come to life

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.580
and that was amazing, it was just amazing.

00:00:42.923 --> 00:00:45.780
VOICE OVER: Eske Willerslev is&nbsp;
a geneticist and palaeontologist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.880
who helped pioneer a technique which&nbsp;
uses ancient DNA to explore the past.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.100
ESKE: If you look around here right, we are&nbsp;
the Zoological Museum in Cambridge, we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:02.580
bones all over the place and this has been the&nbsp;
traditional way people were looking at the past.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.168
But I mean fossils are generally very rare.

00:01:06.168 --> 00:01:10.740
VOICE OVER: Fossilisation requires very&nbsp;
specific conditions to preserve things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.020
like bone and even then palaeontologists&nbsp;
often have to be lucky to find fossils.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.660
DNA on the other hand is everywhere.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.700
ESKE: Every single cell,&nbsp;
right, in the body contains DNA

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.720
and a ground sloth like this or an elephant they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.900
are shedding DNA all the time&nbsp;
to the surroundings, right,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.000
so it's just if I do like this...

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.168
DNA will be on this table for me.

00:01:36.168 --> 00:01:39.660
VOICE OVER: This is called&nbsp;
environmental DNA or 'e-DNA'.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.840
It's been a core part of&nbsp;
Eske's research for decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.420
and is now a thriving field in palaeontology

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.040
but it wasn't always.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.460
ESKE: I remember it was during&nbsp;
my doctorate and I went into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.800
you know, my supervisor and I said&nbsp;
to him, well, I think maybe I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.060
try to test whether DNA from animals and&nbsp;
plants can survive in the soil, right?

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.500
Because I was sitting out looking&nbsp;
at the window and I saw leaves fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.380
down from the trees and a dog&nbsp;
taking a crap on the street.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.780
The question is, what happens to the DNA?

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.620
And I said well maybe it's surviving&nbsp;
in the sediments and he laughed at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.068
me and said I've never heard&nbsp;
anything as stupid in my life.

00:02:16.068 --> 00:02:20.850
VOICE OVER: Now Eske's research involves&nbsp;
looking for DNA preserved in ancient soils.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.720
ESKE: The DNA molecules are electrically charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.000
and some of the sediment particles, you know in&nbsp;
the soil, are electrically charged so DNA will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.888
bind to these sediment particles and that will&nbsp;
reduce the chemical degradation rate of the DNA.

00:02:36.888 --> 00:02:41.520
VOICE OVER: The colder it is the better the DNA&nbsp;
preservation, so one of the best places to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.400
for any ancient DNA is ground that's been&nbsp;
continuously frozen — so-called permafrost.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.960
So in 2006 Eske and colleagues headed to&nbsp;
Greenland in search of frozen ancient dirt.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:02.280
ESKE: It's what we call an Arctic desert.&nbsp;
I mean it's a stony area where you can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.028
see life is really struggling to survive, and&nbsp;
so are you when you are there to be honest!

00:03:08.028 --> 00:03:12.300
VOICE OVER: The team took samples from a&nbsp;
location known as the Kap København Formation.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:18.180
These were cores of permafrost, the oldest&nbsp;
parts of which dated back two million years.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.860
But when they tried looking for&nbsp;
DNA in 2006 they were out of luck.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:27.900
ESKE: We tried with those techniques that were&nbsp;
available at the time and we got nothing, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.560
I mean — and the general notion at that time was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:36.060
well DNA can maximum survive for one&nbsp;
million years. So it wasn't that surprising.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:41.160
But every time we made improvements&nbsp;
in our DNA extraction methods or our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:46.680
sequencing abilities we went back to&nbsp;
the Kap København samples and tried,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.720
right, and we failed and we failed and&nbsp;
we failed — until a couple of years ago

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.348
and suddenly we got a hit.

00:03:54.348 --> 00:03:59.220
VOICE OVER: As the technology developed the team&nbsp;
were able to extract more and more short fragments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:05.400
of DNA bound to the sediment and then to amplify&nbsp;
these using modern day biomolecular techniques.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:11.040
ESKE: At that stage it was like, whoa, we&nbsp;
got DNA out that is two million years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.428
right. The second whoa was, I think,&nbsp;
when we got the animal DNA out.

00:04:16.428 --> 00:04:19.980
VOICE OVER: They were finally able to&nbsp;
piece together the DNA fragments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.160
build a picture of what this ancient&nbsp;
environment might have looked like.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:30.180
ESKE: We had a breakthrough and we could basically&nbsp;
see this, this whole ecosystem come to life.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.700
And that was amazing, it was just amazing.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.300
There was mastodons running around, reindeers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:42.240
geese, hares, also marine animals like&nbsp;
the horseshoe crab — which suggested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.988
a much warmer environment even in the sea&nbsp;
during that time, which was also surprising.

00:04:47.988 --> 00:04:52.740
VOICE OVER: The picture looked very different&nbsp;
from that of modern Greenland. Mastodons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.628
an ancient relative of elephants,&nbsp;
were a particularly unexpected find.

00:04:56.628 --> 00:05:00.840
VOICE OVER: No one had predicted&nbsp;
that mastodon should be around.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.700
It's found in North America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:09.420
and Greenland at that time was also an island,&nbsp;
so it was separated from North America just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:16.020
today, and therefore people didn't think that&nbsp;
animals like mastodons could get there, right.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.780
But it, well it did, right?

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.420
So either it must have been swimming from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.308
Canadian Islands into Greenland or&nbsp;
potentially walked over the sea ice.

00:05:25.308 --> 00:05:28.020
VOICE OVER: Mastodon fossils&nbsp;
have been found in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.380
sites in North America but never in Greenland

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.620
and according to Eske this demonstrates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.340
benefits of using e-DNA to&nbsp;
study ancient ecosystems.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.560
ESKE: Palaeontology obviously you can do&nbsp;
more than just identify the bone right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:49.560
so if you find parts of a skeleton you can&nbsp;
also say things about locomotion for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:55.440
you can say something about whether it was hurt&nbsp;
or damaged you know during life for example.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.320
DNA can't tell you anything about that, but what&nbsp;
DNA can is it gives you information about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:08.100
genetic code itself, it gives us the opportunity&nbsp;
to follow evolution in action basically, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:13.860
you can actually see how species are&nbsp;
genetically evolving and changing.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:20.268
It also gives an opportunity to not only see it&nbsp;
on a species level but a whole ecosystem level.

00:06:20.268 --> 00:06:24.480
VOICE OVER: The use of e-DNA is growing&nbsp;
among palaeontologists as techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.028
improve and Eske is already thinking&nbsp;
about how to extract even older DNA.

00:06:29.028 --> 00:06:34.920
VOICE OVER: I think it's definitely possible to&nbsp;
go back further, I really think so, and I think so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.400
for at least one important reason.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:45.480
We can see that some of the DNA that is&nbsp;
captured in the sediments we are not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:52.320
able at this stage to properly release.&nbsp;
When we become capable of doing that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.140
and we're working on it obviously,&nbsp;
then I think you will target a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:03.348
new pool of DNA and I think that will&nbsp;
bring us significantly further back.

00:07:03.348 --> 00:07:07.800
VOICE OVER: The previous record for&nbsp;
ancient DNA was 1.2 million years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.640
and it belonged to a mammoth&nbsp;
frozen in Siberian permafrost.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.780
So Eske's 2-million-year-old DNA is already a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:19.620
jump — but there may be a limit to&nbsp;
the oldest DNA that can be recovered.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:25.860
Theoretically the oldest permafrost that&nbsp;
could still exist is 2.6 million years old;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.040
before that it was too warm&nbsp;
for permafrost to form.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.700
But it's not just about finding the oldest DNA.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.000
Getting these kind of techniques&nbsp;
to work in different kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.860
environments is another big challenge.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:43.560
ESKE: Obviously in Greenland we got the&nbsp;
sample because it's cold — and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:49.080
good for long-term DNA preservation. So&nbsp;
it is really a good place to start if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:55.740
you want to get DNA that are millions-of-years. 
Whether it will also work out in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:59.280
you can say, other settings, like&nbsp;
temperate settings for example...

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.940
I wouldn't exclude that possibility&nbsp;
at all because temperature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.668
although it is important, is&nbsp;
not the only determining factor.

00:08:07.668 --> 00:08:12.960
VOICE OVER: Of course the study of DNA alone has&nbsp;
its limitations. Without the fossil record we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.620
wouldn't know much about what these animals&nbsp;
actually looked like — but when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:21.840
the more recent past eDNA is an exciting&nbsp;
new part of the palaeontologist's toolkit.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:28.740
ESKE: Environmental DNA is becoming more and&nbsp;
more a tool that people are using in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:36.060
describe communities, right. So I think it will&nbsp;
always be, you can say, a combination of tools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:41.928
And I'm sure also we will in the future see&nbsp;
tools that I can't even imagine now to be honest!

00:08:41.928 --> 00:08:45.600
VOICE OVER: Even now there are many&nbsp;
more ecosystems yet to be explored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:51.000
using environmental DNA and perhaps&nbsp;
many more surprises yet to be uncovered.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.960
ESKE: It's great, I mean, this is&nbsp;
why you do science right? It's for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.400
those moments you know where you're&nbsp;
just like, oh my god. I mean it's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:04.200
it's just changing the way we looked at things.&nbsp;
and this is the dream of a scientist right.

